     <article class="story single" data-beat="ai">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">AI</span> · TechCrunch AI · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Character.AI Files for Bankruptcy After Five Years, Sells Assets to Disney</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Character.AI</span> <span>#Bankruptcy</span> <span>#AI Chatbot</span> <span>#Disney</span></div>
       <p>Character.AI, an interactive chatbot platform that enables users to create and converse with AI characters, has filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy after five years of operation. The startup cited a series of lawsuits and difficulties in scaling its popular platform as key factors. Court documents indicate the company holds assets and liabilities ranging between $50 million and $100 million. As part of its restructuring, Character.AI has agreed to sell some of its assets to Disney for $20 million, alongside granting a non-exclusive perpetual license for its underlying technology.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/characterai-files-for-bankruptcy-after-five-years-sells-asse.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The company was founded in late 2021 by former Google engineers Noam Shazeer, a co-author of the landmark Transformer paper, and Daniel De Freitas. Initially, Character.AI focused on training foundation models and developing its AI chatbot platform. By September 2022, the startup achieved a unicorn valuation following a $150 million fundraise, driven by high demand. At that time, the company reported that its users had exchanged over 2 billion messages with its bots within five months, with active users averaging more than two hours daily on the platform. Its apps also garnered over 1.7 million installs in their first week after launch in May 2023.</p><p>A significant shift occurred in August 2024 when Google secured a $2.7 billion deal to license Character.AI’s models, leading to both Shazeer and De Freitas returning to the search giant. Dominic Perella, the company's general counsel, then stepped in as interim CEO, and the startup pivoted its focus from training foundation models to growth. Despite these changes, the platform maintained strong traction, recording 20 million monthly active users by October of that year.</p><p>However, the company faced considerable challenges, including a lawsuit initiated after the suicide of a 14-year-old Florida boy, whose mother alleged her son became obsessed with a chatbot on the platform. Texas subsequently launched an investigation into the platform’s adherence to child privacy and safety laws. Character.AI settled some of these lawsuits, implemented additional safety features, and banned minors from its platform. In June last year, Karandeep Singh Anand, a former Brex and Meta executive, was appointed CEO, and the company expanded its offerings to include AI video generation, social feeds, interactive storytelling, and microdramas.</p><p>Despite these efforts and significant funding of over $193 million from investors like Lightspeed and a16z, the changes proved insufficient to achieve sustainability. CEO Colin Adams stated that the court-supervised sale process aims to maximize value for stakeholders. He noted that while operations will continue during the sale, expected to close in late November or early December, there is not yet a definitive bid or buyer. Adams expressed hope for a proposal that would allow continued platform operations, but warned that if such a bid is unavailable, the platform would be wound down. This situation highlights the difficulties in scaling and monetizing consumer-facing AI products, where high user engagement often translates to increased AI operational costs. TechCrunch's correspondent Ivan, who reported on this, is based out of India.</p><p>What to watch: The outcome of the asset sale and the future of the Character.AI platform’s operations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is thorough, well-structured, and incorporates all relevant facts and context from the source text.</p>
