     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Colorado River Basin Faces Severe Groundwater Depletion</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Research from Virginia Tech reveals an unprecedented pattern of groundwater loss across the Colorado River Basin. While the Lower Basin historically saw greater declines, the Upper Basin experienced a rapid depletion of 4.6 trillion gallons between 2020 and 2022, nearly double the loss of the Lower Basin.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/colorado-river-basin-faces-severe-groundwater-depletion.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Using NASA satellite data, scientists found that surface-water shortages are increasingly driving groundwater extraction as a buffer. Experts warn that current management strategies must move beyond surface-level monitoring to address the critical decline of groundwater reserves, which threatens the stability of the entire basin's water supply.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a clear summary of the research findings, including the specific figures and the shift in hydrological patterns.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-colorado-river-drought-unprecedented-pattern.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
