     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Escape Rooms Offer Safe Practice for Healthcare Students</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Healthcare Education</span> <span>#Escape Rooms</span> <span>#Occupational Therapy</span> <span>#Clinical Skills</span></div>
       <p>Healthcare education is increasingly utilizing escape rooms to provide students with a safe environment to make decisions and learn from mistakes without real-world consequences. This approach addresses the challenge of preparing students to apply theoretical knowledge to complex, unpredictable patient situations, which rarely conform to textbook case studies. For instance, three occupational therapy students recently made an incorrect decision in an escape room scenario, but no harm was done to a real patient.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/escape-rooms-offer-safe-practice-for-healthcare-students.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Healthcare professionals frequently need to make judgments with incomplete information, adapt to changing priorities, and sometimes rethink their approach as a situation evolves. The Health and Care Professions Council (HCPC) standards for occupational therapists mandate practitioners to use professional judgment, analyze information, solve problems, and make reasoned decisions. Escape rooms are designed to cultivate these critical skills before students' decisions impact actual patients.</p><p>An escape room designed for occupational therapy students involves teams of three who receive simulated emergency department notes. They must identify crucial information, establish connections, and collectively decide on the next steps. When they believe they have the correct answer, they press a buzzer. While it functions as a game, its educational value stems from the required critical thinking and discussion among team members, rather than just the playful aspect.</p><p>Escape rooms are already widely implemented across various healthcare education fields, including medicine, nursing, pharmacy, dentistry, and physical therapy, as evidenced by a systematic review of 52 studies. Key features of these educational tools include teamwork, experiential learning, and post-activity discussions. While some game-based approaches have shown potential in improving student engagement and learning outcomes, rigorous research with strong comparison groups is still needed to conclusively prove their superiority over conventional teaching methods.</p><p>The design of these educational escape rooms prioritizes learning objectives. Uncertainty is a built-in element, where students are not immediately told which information is most relevant. This encourages listening to different interpretations and collaboratively reaching a decision. The buzzer serves to indicate when reasoning has gone in the wrong direction, creating pressure similar to real-life scenarios but without the associated risks.</p><p>The learning process extends beyond the escape itself. Post-game discussions are crucial, allowing students to reflect on their decisions, identify overlooked details, and consider how others' perspectives might have altered their thinking. This reflective practice is also a core component of professional standards for occupational therapists. The methodology aligns with occupational therapy's focus on learning through activity and understanding how 'doing' relates to concepts like 'being, becoming, and belonging.' This approach can be implemented using simple tools like simulated hospital notes, puzzles, and a story, making it accessible for integration into standard class sessions.</p><p>What to watch: Further research on the long-term impact of escape rooms on clinical reasoning skills.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the source text, correctly identifying the educational purpose, methodology, and the balance between gamification and clinical reasoning.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-rooms-health-students-safe-wrong.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
