     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Karnataka High Court Rules Against Denying PPF Interest on Irregular Account After 12 Years</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#PPF</span> <span>#Karnataka High Court</span> <span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#HUF Account</span></div>
       <p>The Karnataka High Court recently delivered a significant ruling, stating that postal authorities could not deny Public Provident Fund (PPF) interest on an account declared irregular after allowing it to operate for 12 years. This decision, dated 31 March 2023, in the case of K. Shankarlal v. The Postmaster HSG I, Malleshwaram Post Office and others (Writ Petition No. 2042 of 2023), highlights the responsibility of authorities for irregularities they have permitted. The specific account, opened on 3 September 2009 in the name of a Hindu Undivided Family (HUF) through an agent at the Malleswaram Post Office in Bengaluru, was found to be irregular because the PPF Scheme had been amended on 13 May 2005 to discontinue the opening of accounts in the name of HUFs.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/karnataka-high-court-rules-against-denying-ppf-interest-on-i.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>K. Shankarlal, the depositor, had intended to build savings for his daughter's marriage. Despite the rule change, postal authorities allowed the account to operate and accepted deposits for 12 years. It was only in September 2021 that they informed Shankarlal the account would be closed without interest due to the irregularity. Challenging this, Shankarlal argued he invested in good faith and was never informed of the rule change nor had the authorities objected to the account during the years they accepted his deposits. The postal authorities, conversely, argued that the account was irregular under the amended scheme, thus interest was not payable.</p><p>The court held that the postal authorities could not accept deposits for 12 years and then penalise the depositor by denying interest for an irregularity they had themselves permitted. It noted that authorities are expected to know the rules governing the PPF scheme and, having allowed the account to operate for years, could not shift the entire responsibility to the depositor. The ruling drew support from the Supreme Court's decision in Bhagwati Vanaspati Traders v. Senior Superintendent of Post Offices, which, in the context of an irregularly issued National Savings Certificate, held that an irregularity did not necessarily justify denying all interest if the defect could be corrected. The Karnataka High Court found the postal authorities' decision unfair and inconsistent with Article 14 of the Constitution, which guarantees equality before the law.</p><p>For an ordinary saver or taxpayer, this ruling is relevant if their account has been allowed to operate by authorities before being declared irregular. It does not mean every irregular PPF account automatically qualifies for full interest. The distinction is crucial, as PPF rules can restrict interest on certain irregular accounts or deposits. For instance, in a separate case, the National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission ruled that excess deposits beyond the permitted annual limit were not entitled to interest, while eligible deposits remained entitled to interest. In Shankarlal's case, the court set aside the communications denying interest and directed authorities to pay interest on the deposits at the rates applicable under the PPF scheme for the relevant period, and then close the account on maturity or in accordance with the scheme's terms. The outcome for other investors will depend on the specific facts and applicable law.</p><p>What to watch: Investors whose PPF accounts are declared irregular should examine the reason, operating period, and applicable rules.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report is comprehensive and accurately reflects all details from the source, including case names, dates, and legal context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
