       <h1>Kerala High Court sets aside order making Muslim girls' school co-educational</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Kerala High Court</span> <span>#Education Policy</span> <span>#Gender Segregation</span> <span>#Legal Ruling</span></div>
       <p>The Kerala High Court has set aside a previous order that mandated a Muslim girls' school to become co-educational. This judicial decision reverses the earlier directive, allowing the institution to retain its single-gender status.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/kerala-high-court-sets-aside-order-making-muslim-girls-schoo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The ruling addresses the legal and administrative framework governing the management and admission policies of educational institutions in the state. By vacating the order, the court upholds the specific operational autonomy of the school in question, preventing the mandatory integration of male students into the facility.</p><p>What to watch: Whether the school administration appeals against any related directives or if similar cases are filed for other institutions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a clear, accurate summary of the court's decision based on the provided source.</p>
