     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Madras High Court Upholds Resignations of Six AIADMK MLAs</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Madras High Court has upheld Tamil Nadu Assembly Speaker J.C.D. Prabhakar’s decision to accept the resignations of six AIADMK MLAs who joined the ruling TVK. The court ruled the resignations were voluntary and complied with the Constitution, noting the Speaker was not required to conduct an extensive inquiry into the members' motives.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/madras-high-court-upholds-resignations-of-six-aiadmk-mlas.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>However, the court directed the Speaker to conclude pending disqualification proceedings against four of those MLAs within two months. The judges clarified that accepting resignations does not render separate disqualification proceedings academic, affirming the Speaker's authority to adjudicate violations of the party whip issued in May 2026.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The summary correctly identifies the court's decision and the specific directions issued to the Speaker regarding disqualification proceedings.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/madras-high-court-upholds-tamil-nadu-assembly-speakers-decision-to-accept-resignations-of-six-aiadmk-mlas/article71577841.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
