     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Planetary systems reveal two distinct families tied to their host stars</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Planetary Systems</span> <span>#Astrophysics</span> <span>#Exoplanets</span> <span>#INAF</span></div>
       <p>An international research team led by the Italian National Institute for Astrophysics (INAF) has discovered that multiplanet systems identified in the Milky Way belong to two distinct “families.” Analyzing a sample of 300 well-characterized planetary systems from over 1,000 known, the study, published in Nature Astronomy, demonstrates a “genetic link” between host stars and their planets that shapes their evolution and stability.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/planetary-systems-reveal-two-distinct-families-tied-to-their.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The first family comprises wide, massive systems, often with total masses between twice and 10 times that of our solar system. These systems orbit stars more massive and richer in metals than the sun and exhibit a high “dynamical temperature,” indicating a past marked by violent gravitational instabilities, giant collisions, and planetary ejections.</p><p>Conversely, the second family includes compact, lower-mass systems with planets of comparable masses and sizes, often described as “peas-in-a-pod.” These orbit stars with mass and metallicity equal to or lower than the sun, typically at distances much smaller than Mercury's. Their dynamical histories show less violence, more closely resembling that of our solar system.</p><p>Diego Turrini, an INAF researcher, explained that stellar properties determine the system's fate, influencing whether it experiences marked instability. Camilla Danielski, a co-first author, noted the challenge of distinguishing real differences from observational limits, confirming that selection effects revealed deeper structural differences. Numerical simulations, supervised by Danae Polychroni, further supported that unstable systems arise from an abundance of material causing planets to form too large.</p><p>What to watch: How this genetic link guides the search for solar system-like architectures and potentially habitable planets.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: An excellent, dense summary of the scientific study that captures the methodology, the two distinct families, and the researchers' conclusions perfectly.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-planetary-reveal-distinct-families-host.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
