     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-10-13 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Startup Licube Aims to Supply Purified Lithium-6 for Fusion Power Plants</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Startup Licube has licensed technology from Japan's Hirosaki University to purify lithium-6 from natural lithium to serve the emerging fusion power industry. Fusion power plants require lithium blankets surrounding reactors to capture fast-moving neutrons and generate rare tritium fuel. Because lithium-6 makes up only about 7.5% of natural lithium and is difficult to separate, Licube targets a potential $50 billion market by 2040. Until fusion becomes mainstream, Licube plans to apply its ceramic membrane electrolyzer technology to purify lithium for semiconductors, pharmaceuticals, fission reactors, and spacecraft.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/startup-licube-aims-to-supply-purified-lithium-6-for-fusion-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent density; the brief covers the technology, the market potential, and the interim business strategy within the word limit.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/13/licube-wants-to-be-the-fuel-provider-to-the-fusion-power-industry-see-how-at-techcrunch-disrupt/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
