     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-13 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>What if AI boom busts? IMF clarifies</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Business</span> <span>#IMF</span> <span>#AI</span> <span>#Banking</span></div>
       <p>The International Monetary Fund (IMF) has warned that more global banks could be pushed to the brink in a &quot;doomsday scenario&quot; this year compared to a year ago. The IMF indicated that AI-related challenges have the potential to imperil banks, even as profits in the sector continue to soar.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/what-if-ai-boom-busts-imf-clarifies.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The IMF's latest stress test specifically examined how global banks would cope with a combination of an AI crash, persistently soaring government bond yields, and a broader economic slowdown. Under such a severe scenario, a significant portion of the global banking system would be affected.</p><p>The analysis revealed that 22% of global banking assets would be held in 110 weak lenders. For these institutions, their core capital ratio would fall below 7%, or a higher threshold designated for the world's most complex banks. This marks an increase from last year's test, which found 18% of banking assets in 88 weak lenders under similar stressed conditions.</p><p>Athanasios Vamvakidis of the IMF noted that the scenarios for each year reflect risks considered pertinent at the time, developed using a common methodology. He highlighted that risks have amplified this year across the global economy, citing energy supply disruption, growing fears of an AI bubble, and rising yields. Vamvakidis stressed, &quot;It's a reminder that we shouldn't take resilience as a given,&quot; and suggested the analysis should serve as counsel for authorities contemplating regulatory changes, especially given a wave of deregulation that has propelled banks to record profits and substantial shareholder payouts.</p><p>What to watch: Policymakers' response to IMF's counsel on regulatory changes.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively communicates the IMF's findings, including the specific stress test metrics and the context provided by the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/world-news/what-if-ai-boom-busts-22-of-assets-would-be-with-110-weak-lenders-imf/articleshow/134926069.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
