     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-13 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Xbox Embraces AI-Powered Game Ports, Launches New Labs Group</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Xbox</span> <span>#AI</span> <span>#Gaming</span> <span>#Modding</span></div>
       <p>Microsoft's Xbox is officially embracing the &quot;chaos&quot; of a new AI-powered era of game modding, acknowledging a &quot;wild time&quot; in retro gaming. This comes as decompilations and recompilations enable gamers to port classic titles to new devices or merge code from two different games into novel experiences. Microsoft has observed examples such as skateboarding being modded into Call of Duty and Halo being played directly in a web browser.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-13/xbox-embraces-ai-powered-game-ports-launches-new-labs-group.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>To navigate this rapidly emerging landscape, Microsoft is establishing a new Xbox Labs group. Xbox CEO Asha Sharma detailed that this &quot;small group of game makers, engineers and scientists&quot; will focus on transforming &quot;emerging ideas into things our players and developers can experience.&quot; The group's operational philosophy involves building &quot;in the open,&quot; learning from user activities, and measuring success by increased player engagement and creation.</p><p>Sharma believes the recent experiments, including Windows PC builds translated to run on Apple Silicon or ported to run inside browsers, &quot;challenge long-held assumptions about our industry.&quot; These include the fundamental &quot;tether between software and hardware,&quot; the &quot;boundary between creation and play,&quot; and traditional methods of game discovery and experience. She stated that while predicting gaming's future is difficult, &quot;making great games easier to access, giving old games new life and allowing more players to become creators and co-creators&quot; are &quot;unmistakably good outcomes for players,&quot; even if they &quot;challenge our existing business models.&quot;</p><p>Modders are increasingly undertaking tasks that Microsoft has historically performed through its Xbox backward compatibility program, which allows old games to run on modern hardware. However, these community efforts operate without the &quot;business model restrictions&quot; that bind Microsoft. Sharma explicitly sees a significant opportunity for AI agent use within Xbox, noting coding was the &quot;first major workload for agents because they can write, run, and test software.&quot; She highlighted that games share many of these properties, with players spending &quot;more than 72 billion hours a year playing XBOX games,&quot; which is &quot;roughly five times the estimated time spent writing code worldwide.&quot;</p><p>Xbox Labs is positioned as a strategic avenue for Microsoft to experiment with how AI will shape where games are played. The company intends to share any developed tools and acquired learnings with developers across the industry. Despite this forward-looking approach, a critical question arises regarding Microsoft's response to decompiles and recompiles of games where modders make its &quot;precious IP&quot; freely available, a scenario that could challenge existing intellectual property frameworks. Microsoft declined to comment on unauthorized IP use when asked for publication.</p><p>What to watch: Xbox's balancing act between fostering innovation and safeguarding intellectual property.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive summary of the source, capturing the launch of Xbox Labs and the strategic rationale behind it.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/news/1010406/xbox-labs-game-decompiling-emulation-exploration" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
